    "title": "Hypothetical Evidence for Local LLM Privacy Improvements: Prompt Isolation",
    "body": "This post explores a hypothetical scenario for evaluating whether local LLM deployments enhance user privacy. The core idea is to isolate prompts, ensuring they never leave the local machine. This direct control over data flow is a primary theoretical benefit of local LLMs compared to cloud-based services.\n\nTo establish evidence for this, a controlled experiment would be needed. Key metrics would include verifying that prompts are processed entirely offline. Furthermore, a crucial step involves auditing system logs to confirm no prompt data is inadvertently transmitted. Any optional network connections used by the LLM framework itself would also require careful examination to ensure they do not exfiltrate prompt content or associated metadata.\n\nIt's vital to acknowledge the limitations of this hypothetical setup. No formal security audit or compliance certification has been performed. Therefore, while prompt isolation is a strong theoretical advantage, this scenario does not provide definitive proof of enhanced privacy. Discussion is invited on what constitutes sufficient evidence beyond prompt containment.",
