    "title": "Assessing Local LLM Privacy: Beyond Prompt Containment - A Hypothetical Analysis",
    "body": "Investigating the privacy benefits of local LLM deployments requires a multi-faceted approach, even in a hypothetical experimental context. While keeping prompts on the local machine (F1) is a foundational step, it's not the sole determinant of privacy improvement.\n\nTo build a case for enhanced privacy, the hypothetical experiment must extend to scrutinizing system behavior. This includes a thorough review of all generated logs for any potential data leakage. Additionally, any network connections, even those intended for non-prompt-related functions (e.g., model updates, error reporting), must be meticulously examined to ensure they do not transmit sensitive information. The absence of a security audit or compliance certification (F3) means these checks are paramount for forming an initial assessment.\n\nThis hypothetical approach highlights that evidence for privacy gains relies on verifying the *entire* data lifecycle, not just prompt handling. What other mechanisms or checks would be essential to consider in a real-world scenario, given these limitations? Discussion is welcome on practical verification strategies.",
